SCR Structure
Situation
The System Is Heavily Customized, and Its Problems Aren't Yet Visible
Over more than a decade, the system has been heavily customized, built largely on custom versions of capabilities Salesforce now provides out of the box.
Running on those custom versions, it misses built-in features and the improvements Salesforce delivers over time, and every custom piece is maintained by hand.
What's documented describes how the system works, not deeply enough to reveal its problems.
For example, the documents lay out the data model but not how it's been customized, so the problems in it stay out of view.
Its problems surface only when work runs into them, so the full set can't be seen in advance.
(Spoken, not on the slide: we've seen this in the first area we examined and expect the same elsewhere.)
Knowledge of the System Is Fragmented With No Common Reference Across Stakeholders
Business knowledge of what the system should do sits with OCFP; technical knowledge of how it's built sits with the support team.
OCIO, OCFP, and the support team don't share an agreed picture of the current state, so each works from a partial view.
That knowledge lives in people rather than a maintained record, so it's incomplete today and leaves when the team changes.
Three High-Stakes Changes Are Imminent, and Each One Depends on Understanding the System
Coordinating the Lightning upgrade depends on identifying the dependencies it touches and aligning the parties and systems it has to work around.
The new backup and recovery solution captures data automatically, but a correct recovery depends on knowing the dependencies, automation, and integrations that make the system work.
Reworking the data architecture can't be planned without knowing what the current custom build does and what depends on it.
Complication
Without a Baseline, the Full Cost of Every High-Stakes Initiative Is Invisible Until It Is Too Late
Today the true cost of each initiative is a guess, because nothing maps the work it actually involves.
The dependencies that drive the real cost stay hidden until the work is underway, so the true scope often becomes clear only after the plan is set.
That raises the risk on the high-stakes work ahead, which the system has to take on while still running the business.
Without a Foundation, Every Change Carries Risk and Decisions Rest on Assumptions
(Spoken open, verbatim: "You are about to do high-stakes things to a system nobody fully understands, and without this foundation, those changes carry real risk.")
Without visibility into the dependencies, there's no way to judge the risk of a change before making it.
With no safety net, a breaking change can go unnoticed until it reaches production.
Without a shared, documented reference, discussions turn on whoever knows the system best, not on facts everyone can see.
