Group outcome: the system can change without raising the chance of an unplanned outage or unbounded scope.
Inventorying the Physical Components Makes the As-Built System Visible and Verified
Outcome: Establishes a single authoritative source for how the system is actually built, its dependencies, automation, and integrations, kept under version control, in place of knowledge that today is partial and lives in people.
What it delivers
Sized on fact, not guesswork. With the baseline in place, each initiative can be broken down and costed on what the work actually involves.
Conversations on facts. A shared, documented reference levels the field, so discussions aren't decided by whoever happens to know the system best.
Stays current, not another stale document. Under version control, every change to the record is tracked and it keeps pace with the system.
Build knowledge survives team changes. What today lives in people, and leaves when the team turns over, is held in a maintained record, removing a single point of failure.
What it covers and how
The inventory catalogs the org's components: objects, fields, Visualforce pages, Apex classes, workflow rules, etc.
Draft in about a week, then verified by OCIO, then by OCFP.
A Well-Architected Review Surfaces Hidden Risks and Ranks What to Fix
Outcome: Surfaces the system's hidden problems and critical risks before the upcoming changes run into them, and returns a prioritized list of what to fix.
What it delivers
A structured review against proven standards. A well-architected review answers a set of foundational questions to identify the system's critical issues and the areas that could be improved, and returns a set of recommended actions.
It reveals what isn't visible today. The review uncovers previously unknown issues and hidden architectural debt, the long-standing design problems that stay invisible until a change runs into them, and shows how well the system aligns with best practices.
Risks found before the one-way doors. Critical issues surface before the upcoming changes lock in choices that are hard to reverse.
Repeatable as the system changes. The review can be re-run as the architecture evolves, so the picture stays honest through the Lightning upgrade and the migration.
What it covers and how
The review rates the system on security, reliability, performance, and how easily it can change, against a recognized standard.
Findings delivered as a ranked report, then walked through with OCIO.
Business-Driven Test Cases Catch a Breaking Change Before It Reaches Production
Outcome: Reduce the likelihood and severity of a breaking change reaching production, by checking before release that the system still does what the business needs.
What it delivers
Tests written from what the business needs, not from the code. Each test states a result the business expects, so the system is checked in business terms rather than technical ones.
A breaking change is caught before release. When a change would stop the system doing what the business depends on, the tests flag it before it reaches production, instead of after consumers feel it.
Confidence to make the upcoming changes. Because the tests are tied to business behavior rather than how the system is built, re-running them after the Lightning upgrade or the migration confirms that what the business relies on still works, even as the implementation changes.
A path to automated testing that lasts beyond the upgrade. The work evaluates what should be automated and delivers a recommendation, so the safety net runs on every change at low effort throughout ongoing operations, not just once for the Lightning upgrade.
What it covers and how
The core business processes the system supports, prioritized by impact and by what the upcoming changes touch.
An evaluation of testing automation needs and a recommendation the program can adopt for ongoing operations.
Expected behavior defined with OCFP, written up as tests, then run before each release.
Revising the O&M Plan Makes Its Commitments Accurate and Verifiable
Outcome: Revises the existing O&M Plan so it describes how the system is actually operated, secured, monitored, and recovered, in terms that can be verified, in place of assertions that are inaccurate or can't be confirmed.
What it delivers
A plan that matches the system as it runs. Descriptions are corrected to the current system, so the plan can be relied on rather than describing tools and processes that aren't actually in use.
Claims that can be verified. Each control states how it is confirmed and how often, so "the system is secure" becomes something that can be checked and audited, not just asserted.
Procedures someone can follow under pressure. Incident response, monitoring, and recovery carry the real steps, owners, and frequency, so the plan can be executed, not just read.
Monitoring tied to how the system is actually watched. The plan defines what is monitored, by whom, and how often, and how security events flow into the SIEM the client already runs, so problems are detected and routed instead of missed.
What it covers and how
Revises the weak sections of the existing plan: security, version control and deployment, incident management, and monitoring and performance.
Revised in place, building on the existing plan, with each assertion replaced by an accurate description and the procedure that backs it.
Question (transition into Discussion)
Which Pending Requests Do We Change on Today's System, and Which Fold Into the Modernization?
(No bullets. The question stands alone to mark the turn from the foundation to the decisions the work in hand forces.)
Discussion
Each Pending Request Is a Choice to Build It Now or Build It Once
Pending item	Risk of building it now	Risk of folding it into the modernization
International address support	Adds to the system's custom debt, and if the modernization moves to standard address handling, the build and its data are redone	International addresses stay unsupported until the modernization, which may be a long wait
Age-related security vulnerabilities	A fix tied to the legacy build may be discarded when the rebuild removes the cause, so part of the effort is spent twice	The exposure stays open until the rebuild ships, and security rarely tolerates a long wait
External API tied to the data architecture	If the data model changes, the API is rebuilt against the new model, so it is built twice and against a moving target	The integration waits for the data-architecture decision, so the API consumer is held up
Caption: The choice turns on whether waiting costs more than building it twice.
